# Kavacha patch 0060 — the ⚙ menu was a list, not a menu. Collapse it.
#
# SYMPTOM, as reported: "when you click on the settings button the dropdown menu
# has too many options ... unclog it in a way that it is clean and minimalistic
# but still retains most info/options."
#
# WHAT IT WAS: patch 0030 generates the panel from the command registry, which
# is right and stays — a hand-maintained menu drifts from the registry the first
# time a plugin registers anything. What was wrong is that it rendered the
# result as ONE FLAT LIST: Settings, then a dim domain header, then every
# available command, then the next header, and so on. Measured on the reporter's
# own profile that is 22 command rows + 6 headers + Settings, and the panel came
# out ~720px tall in a 758px window. Patches 0037 and 0040 are both scars from
# this — 0037 added a scrolling box because the tail was unreachable, 0040
# re-tightened the cap after the anchor moved. Neither was the actual defect.
# You cannot read 28 options; you can only search them, and there was no search.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOW. Nothing was removed — every command the registry offers is
# still one or two interactions away — but the same commands arrive in three
# shapes instead of one:
#
#   1. Settings stays PINNED at the top. It is what the gear looks like it does,
#      so it must not be behind a disclosure.
#   2. Each domain collapses to ONE row carrying its command count: "Appearance
#      9". The count is the half of collapsing that keeps the information — a
#      closed section still says how much is behind it. Opening a section closes
#      the previous one, so the panel is never taller than its largest single
#      domain.
#   3. A FILTER FIELD searches every domain at once, focused the moment the panel
#      opens. This is the part that makes collapsing cheap rather than annoying:
#      if you know what you want, typing three letters beats both the accordion
#      AND the old flat list.
#
# THE ONE DESIGN DECISION WORTH ARGUING WITH: the accordion and the filter are
# the SAME nodes, not two renderings of the registry. Filtering expands every
# section and hides the rows that miss, rather than building a second flat list
# of matches. Two node sets would mean two places for a row to be stale, and
# dropping the grouping the instant you type would throw away the only context
# that tells you a match is an Appearance command rather than a Privacy one.
#
# WHY NOT A PREF, AND WHY NOT A DISK FILE: the open section is remembered in
# module state for the session and nowhere else. It is not a setting — it is
# which part of the menu you happened to be using — and writing it to a pref
# would sync it, back it up, and survive a profile reset for no benefit.
#
# WHY NOT AN OVERLAY: the panel's contents are BUILT IN JS, in KavachaMenu
# .sys.mjs, from the registry, at every popupshowing. There is no markup for a
# chrome CSS overlay to restyle and no static list for it to trim; a CSS-only
# attempt could hide rows but could not add a filter, could not count what it
# hid, and could not put back what it hid. This is the same file patch 0030
# introduced, so it is a patch either way.
#
# ONE NON-OBVIOUS CSS FACT, recorded because getting it wrong silently un-does
# the whole feature: collapsing and filtering both work by setting the `hidden`
# attribute, and every class involved sets an explicit `display`. A class rule
# beats the UA's [hidden] rule, so without the explicit `[hidden] { display:
# none }` block added here, `hidden` sets an attribute and changes nothing on
# screen. Any new class in this panel that sets `display` belongs in that list.
#
# ACCESSIBILITY: the rows live in an HTML container rather than a <menupopup>,
# so none of a menu's keyboard behaviour arrives for free — and did not, before
# this patch either. Added: rows and section headers are focusable, Up/Down walk
# the visible ones and wrap back into the filter, Enter in the filter takes the
# first match, Escape clears a non-empty filter before it closes the panel.
# Section headers are html:button with aria-expanded (an html:button rather than
# a XUL toolbarbutton because a header carries three pieces of content and
# MozToolbarbutton owns its own anonymous children).
#
# VERIFIED LIVE over Marionette against this build, on a 758px-tall window with
# the panel anchored at y=94 — not by reading the diff:
#
#   * resting panel 291px tall, 7 rows, scrollbox scrollHeight == clientHeight
#     (nothing to scroll) — against ~720px and scrolling before.
#   * six sections present with live counts 3/4/3/9/1/1, all aria-expanded
#     false, chevrons at rotate(-45deg).
#   * expanding each section in turn: bottom edge 423–679 in a 758px window, so
#     EVERY section fits without scrolling; opening a second section closes the
#     first (rowsVisible 5, not 14).
#   * filter "theme" -> 1 row, Appearance auto-expanded, other five sections
#     hidden with count 0. Filter "settings" -> the pinned row survives, which
#     is the one query that would otherwise find nothing. Filter "zzzz" -> 0
#     rows and the empty state. Clearing -> back to the 291px resting state.
#   * focus lands in the filter on popupshown; ArrowDown/ArrowUp walk
#     filter -> Settings -> Navigation and back into the filter, each with
#     defaultPrevented true.
#   * min-width 340px was chosen from measurement, not taste: five of the six
#     sections fit inside it, so opening one usually no longer resizes the
#     panel. At the old 280px every section resized it.
#
# Files: src/zen/common/sys/KavachaMenu.sys.mjs (panel construction),
# src/zen/common/styles/kavacha-menu.inc.css (sections, filter, [hidden]
# guards), locales/en-US/browser/browser/zen-command-palette.ftl (two new keys
# — note these reach the app only via bootstrap.sh's sync_locales, since
# `surfer import` does not touch the top-level locales/ tree).
